i='TCS-2799';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='ICALP 2022, article 41: Section 5.4 and Conjecture 5.2. The full version arXiv:2108.06049v3 (21 February 2022), Section 9.4, Conjecture 9.4, retains the same unspecified “no-OGP” premise.'
refs.append(dict(id='regular2023',title='Local algorithms for maximum cut and minimum bisection on locally treelike regular graphs of large degree',authors='Ahmed El Alaoui, Andrea Montanari, Mark Sellke',year=2023,url='https://doi.org/10.1002/rsa.21149',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.06813v2',locator='Revised preprint, 3 February 2023, corresponding to Random Structures & Algorithms: Definition 1.1, Assumption 1 and Theorem 1.7. Assumption 1 concerns the SK Parisi minimizer; the theorem requires the degree to exceed a threshold depending on accuracy/locality.'))
issue='Conjecture 5.2 does not specify the proposition meant by a “no-OGP” assumption: the surrounding discussion invokes the SK-model hypothesis for the large-degree theorem, then separately asks whether finite-degree random regular graphs have an overlap gap. It does not state a finite-degree analogue or an equivalence between these hypotheses. It also does not formally restrict the algorithm to AMP updates despite the conjecture heading. A completed conditional benchmark must fix the premise and the intended algorithm class; deleting the premise would strengthen the source question.'
save(i,dict(
 title='Near-optimal Max-Cut on random regular graphs at every fixed degree',
 formal='Under the intended no-overlap-gap hypothesis, does there exist a classical algorithm, polynomial in n and 1/ε, that for every fixed integer d≥3 and every fixed ε∈(0,1) finds a cut with at least (1−ε)MaxCut(G) edges with high probability when G is a random d-regular graph on n vertices? The degree is fixed independently of the desired accuracy; it cannot be required to grow when ε decreases. The source does not fully specify the conditional hypothesis or whether only AMP algorithms are admissible.',
 definitions='For n>d with nd even, take G uniformly from all simple undirected d-regular graphs on the labeled vertex set [n]. An assignment σ∈{−1,+1}ⁿ defines a cut of size cut_G(σ)=|{{i,j}∈E(G):σᵢ≠σⱼ}|, and MaxCut(G)=max_σ cut_G(σ). The input is the complete adjacency-list representation and an accuracy parameter; the output assigns a sign to each vertex. Accuracy may equivalently be supplied by an integer m≥2 with ε=1/m, avoiding any unlimited-precision numeric input. Polynomial in n and 1/ε means a bound C_d(n+m)^{a_d} for constants independent of n,m; this records the fixed-degree reading of the source. The success probability, over both the graph and internal algorithmic randomness, must tend to 1 as n→∞ through admissible orders, for each fixed d and ε. No guarantee is required on every individual d-regular graph. The overlap of two assignments σ,τ is R(σ,τ)=n^{-1}Σᵢσᵢτᵢ. An overlap-gap condition excludes a fixed interval of overlaps between sufficiently good assignments; its exact thresholds, randomness and coupling conventions form part of a mathematical hypothesis. The paper’s definitions for diluted k-spin systems do not by themselves specify the no-gap hypothesis for this fixed-degree Max-Cut conjecture. AMP means approximate message passing; a precise class of allowed update rules is not supplied in the conjecture.',
 question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Fix the exact no-gap premise and algorithm class intended by the source, then prove or refute that conditional near-optimal approximation claim for every fixed d≥3 with polynomial dependence on n and 1/ε. A theorem only for sufficiently large d depending on ε, a fixed approximation ratio below one, or numerical performance on finitely many graphs does not settle the target.',
 context_blocks=[b('Accuracy at fixed degree is the missing range','The existing conditional algorithm is nearly optimal when the degree is sufficiently large. This question asks for arbitrarily high accuracy while keeping a particular degree, such as d=3, fixed.','primary'),b('The probability guarantee concerns typical graphs','The algorithm may fail on an exceptional set of graphs whose probability vanishes with n. Its approximation ratio is measured against the optimum of the same sampled graph.','primary'),b('The source hypothesis is not just a slogan','The large-degree result uses a specific condition: strict increase of the zero-temperature SK Parisi minimizer. The source’s conjecture instead says “under a no-OGP assumption” without defining the intended fixed-degree premise. Different notions of overlap gaps cannot be substituted without justification.','regular2023'),b('The revised theorem still has a degree threshold','Theorem 1.7 of the 2023 version has a degree threshold depending on its accuracy and locality parameters. Its limit statement therefore does not supply arbitrary accuracy for each fixed small degree.','regular2023'),b('Remaining formulation issue',issue)],
 why='Would clarify how the geometry of near-optimal cuts controls efficient optimization in sparse random graphs, and provide a sharper classical benchmark for proposed quantum optimization advantages.',references=refs,
 progress=[dict(date='2022',text='Conjecture 5.2 proposes polynomial-time near-optimal cuts at every fixed degree under an unspecified no-OGP premise.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2023-02-03',text='The revised large-degree theorem retains a threshold on degree depending on accuracy and locality, under its precise SK-model hypothesis.',citation='regular2023')],status='source_open',status_note='Checked the conference question, full February 2022 version and revised February 2023 large-degree theorem. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no result meeting the full fixed-degree, arbitrary-accuracy target. The original premise remains underspecified.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,
 statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Recovered the no-OGP premise and fixed-degree target, without inventing the missing hypothesis.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Connects solution-space geometry to near-exact optimization of sparse random graphs across all fixed degrees. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Restored the omitted conditional premise and distinguished accuracy at fixed degree from a large-degree limit. Recorded both the hypothesis and algorithm-class ambiguities instead of replacing the source by an unconditional theorem.',
 working_summary=dict(sentences=['The problem concerns almost-maximum cuts in uniformly random regular graphs.','For each fixed degree, the desired algorithm must reach every fixed accuracy in time polynomial in graph size and inverse accuracy.','The source states this conditionally on a no-overlap-gap premise that it does not fully define.','The known large-degree theorem has a specific SK hypothesis and still requires degree to increase with the desired precision.','The hypothesis and intended AMP restriction must be settled before this becomes a complete conditional benchmark.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','regular2023'])
 ),notes=['Recovered the omitted no-OGP condition and fixed-degree quantifiers.','Defined the random graph, approximation ratio, probability and accuracy conventions.','Checked the later full versions and kept the large-degree theorem separate.','Recorded the missing precise hypothesis and intended algorithm class.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.06049','https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06813'],state='improved_unfinished')
